WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:05.000
Yes, I am standing somewhere windswept
 and talking about infrastructure.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.360
I have a style!

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.560
Also, that whooshing sound
you can hear?

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.880
That's the other turbine
 that you can't see in this camera shot.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.280
Anyway. Power grids!
 They have to balance supply and demand.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.040
I've talked about that a few times
 in the past here. But as a quick catch up,

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:21.080
if there's not enough power
 being generated, things break.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.600
But if there's too much power
 being generated, things also break.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.280
And arguably in a worse way. 

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:27.520
There isn't a battery in the world 

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.440
big enough to deal with the daily peaks
 and troughs of national demand.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.840
So power grid engineers have to make sure
 that the power being generated

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.400
is always close to the power
 that's being used.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.440
There are a lot of possible technologies
 to help with that balance.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.760
And I've talked about some of those
 before here, too. 

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.960
Pumped storage that uses a lake
 as a giant battery, or even the big

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.280
lithium-ion setup in Australia
 that's only caught on fire once so far.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.200
But all of those technologies
 assume that supply and demand

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.760
will balance each other out over time,
 usually over a few hours.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.280
What happens if you always
 have too much power?

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.600
Welcome to the Orkney Islands, in the far
 north east of Scotland.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.200
About 22,000 people live here, on around
 1,000 windswept square kilometers.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:12.120
And they have so much renewable energy
 from wind and waves and tides

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.120
that it's genuinely a problem. 

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.960
Solution number one is:
turn off the turbines.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:24.040
- 2013, we were producing
 up to 130% of our electrical needs.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.360
So there can be quite a lot of curtailment
 on several turbines.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.400
There's active network management. 

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.600
The wind turbine will be shut down
 because the network can't handle the load.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.400
There's been a moratorium
 on any new grid connections.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.120
There is potential for some
 microgeneration, but nothing of any scale.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.600
So when you're in a place like Orkney,
 where you have some of the best

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.440
generation potential,
 it's a complete waste.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.480
- Turning off the turbines
 isn't a good solution. It's wasteful.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.200
Depending on the time of day and year, 

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.160
these islands need between
7 and 46 megawatts.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.080
It's almost always windy here, so
a couple of big commercial wind farms

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.520
like this can produce all the power
that folks here need.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.760
But they've also got 500
 domestic turbines, privately owned.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.680
1 in 12 households
 generate their own power. 

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.880
Because why wouldn't you? 

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.200
Once you pay the installation costs, 

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.920
you'll probably turn a profit,
year after year after year.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.760
One of the things I struggled with,
writing this script. is

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.840
that there is so much
going on up here. 

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.160
There's an electric hybrid plane
being tested at the airport.

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.120
There are tidal power experiments,
wave power experiments.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.880
Every type of renewable energy,
almost, is being tested here.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.600
...there's not much solar. 

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.640
And there's a backup:
 for the very rare days

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.840
when the wind is calm,
 there are interconnector cables

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.000
that can pull up to 40 megawatts
 from the Scottish mainland.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.040
But OK: they're generating
 a massive surplus of power,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.600
and they're connected
 to the British National Grid.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.160
So surely solution number two is:
use those interconnectors?

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:48.360
They can just sell the extra power
 back to the mainland.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.480
Well, those 40 megawatts
 that the interconnectors can handle?

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.800
That's not actually all that much.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.960
- The interconnector cables
 we've got are small.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.320
We reckon there's 2,000 megawatts of tidal
 and wave generation around here.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.840
We reckon there's 17,000
 megawatts of offshore wind

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.480
within a couple of hundred
 kilometers of here. 

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.280
The interconnector was built 

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:10.120
for an old day when you used to burn coal
 and send us electricity.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.880
That's gone. We don't do that anymore. 

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.640
We now need to be in a position where
 we've got a better way to get the power

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.160
from these islands away. 

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.760
- If the new interconnector is built,
 it carry enough power

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.640
for all the turbines that Orkney could
 reasonably put up in the next few years.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.800
They could power most of
the highlands of Scotland. 

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:31.040
But building that new interconnector
 will cost a quarter of a billion pounds.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.960
National government requires
 that the islands approve plans for enough

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.280
new turbines to make sure it's
 going to be used, before it's approved.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.280
That's all still
 in the balance politically. 

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.880
So solution number three is that the
islands could start using more electricity. 

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.800
There's already a larger
share of electric cars here

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.280
than anywhere else in Scotland.
But most vehicles still use petrol or diesel.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.480
That could change.
 There's still a lot of oil heating.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.440
The ferries, vital for getting around,
 still burn fossil fuels.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:57.720
The transition is a slow process. 

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.560
No one's going to scrap a
perfectly good car or heater - or ferry -

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.840
unless they have to. 

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:02.920
But there are plans. 

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:08.160
And Orkney's also had  a hydrogen fuel system for years,
 to help soak up a bit of the excess power.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.560
We're producing hydrogen from,
 effectively, spare electricity.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.360
That's why we started. 

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.120
We're taking electricity,
 putting it into fresh water,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.240
and it splits the water
 into hydrogen and oxygen.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.560
We're getting rid of the oxygen
 and we're just capturing the hydrogen.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.920
We're compressing it,
 and then we're using it in this fuel cell.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.680
We have ferries that operate
 within the islands.  One of those ferries 

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.600
is able to be powered by this fuel cell
 when it ties up alongside at night.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.480
And it saves it running its own engines, 

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.880
or it saves it drawing
 power from the grid.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.640
This is just the start of something. 

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.520
This equipment is experimental
 and believe me, it's not efficient.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:41.600
This is not the best way of doing it. 

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.240
But we're finding our way
 into new territory.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.480
We try and unearth 

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:50.640
some of the challenges so that when we
 scale this equipment up, then

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:51.680
those faults have been polished out. 

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.240
- These islands are years
 ahead of the rest of the world.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.160
This is the test bed. 

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.800
This is a small version of the changes
 that the whole planet is going to make.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.720
The transition to renewables 

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.920
is happening faster here in Orkney than,
 I think, anywhere else in the world.

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.680
But as you travel around these islands,
 it just seems normal.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.360
Nothing's visibly changed, but the lessons

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.040
that they're learning here will hopefully
 pave the way for the rest of the world.

